---

pancake has one protocol implementation and several APIs. Choose the one that
matches your language and runtime.

[One core under every API, so the entry point you choose does not change what a session puts on the wire.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-surfaces.svg'))

| API | You write | Reach for it when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [Zig package]($link.page('start')) | Zig | You want the full engine and its types. |
| [C ABI](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/consuming-from-c.md) | C / any FFI | You are embedding, or wrapping a language with no binding yet. |
| [Bindings]($link.page('bindings')) | Rust, TypeScript, Python, Racket | You already work in one of those four. |
| [CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')) | shell / MCP | You want call, host, REPL, or agent MCP (`zig build`, then `zig-out/bin/pancake`). |

The TypeScript binding also compiles to WebAssembly, so the same session runs in
the browser ([In the browser]($link.page('bindings/browser'))).
[Start building]($link.page('start')) walks the Zig round-trip; each row above
links to the corresponding setup guide.

## One engine

A Zig peer, a C consumer, and any binding hold the same OCapN conversation byte
for byte. Which codec and CapTP version you speak comes from negotiation with
the partner, not from which API you use
([Concepts]($link.page('concepts'))). How peers meet on the wire is a separate
choice: [Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')).
[What you can depend on]($link.page('stability')) separates checked behavior
from areas that still need external evidence.
